/*****************************************************************************
	This is the tester incantation. It is a working file for testing new features
    under development so it changes when there is a new feature in the works.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Utilities list;

register(C1Tester);
define
    cv=5;
    n=3;
    lim=8;
    qa=2;
    qb=10;
    ok=1;
    done=0;
    r=0;
    blkBody code={ return argument * 2; };
    fId code={ return argument; };
    c1Run code={
        blk := blkBody;
        cerr "C1 begin":;
        r = *blk(cv) + n * 2 > fId(qa + qb) - lim && ok || !done;
        cerr "C1 r = " r:;
        };
    ;
c1Run();
cerr "C1 SENTINEL":;

stop();

/***************************************************************************
    C1 tester (Clod, 2026-10-07, SEQ 320) -- for the expr-accum branch

    TO RUN IN XCODE
      Both repos on expr-accum (Groups and support). No retok needed.
      Scheme Groups, Run:
        Arguments          IncantForms/WorkingOn/tester
        Working directory  the Groups directory
        Environment        INCANT_EXPR_ACCUM=1     the candidate (unset or 0: today's road)
                           INCANT_ACCUM_TRACE=r    trace expressions whose first item is r
      Breakpoints, each hit ONCE (c1Run's line is the only traced expression):
        accTraceFlat   (GroupRules.mm:3473)  the FLAT list, before the split -- inspect flat
        accBuilt       (GroupRules.mm:3483)  the TIER TREE, finished, not run -- inspect node
      accBuild pops every item off flat, so flat is EMPTY by the time accBuilt stops.
      In lldb:  expr (void)accTraceTree(node, 0)   prints the tree to the console
                p node->groupBody->tag   then node->get(1), node->get(3) ...
      The console also carries ACCUM FLAT and ACCUM TREE lines to compare against.

    Expected tree: acA [r, =, acOr [acAnd [acK [acX, >, acX], &&, ok], ||, acU [!, done]]]
    Expected value: blkBody(5) 10, + n 13, * 2 26; fId(12) - lim 4; 26 > 4 true;
    && ok true; || !done is not evaluated; r reads 1.

    blk is a HOLDER of an action with a code body: *blk is the action, so *blk(cv)
    calls blkBody with 5. A star on a field that holds no group is null by ruling,
    which is why blk is not itself the action.

    THE RULED LINE, r = *blk(cv) + n * 2 > lim - fId(qa + qb) && ok || !done;
    DOES NOT PARSE ON EITHER ROAD (measured 2026-10-07): - is a UnaryOPS member,
    so InvokeArg's UnaryXP alternative absorbs - fId into lim's term, and the
    call's (qa + qb) is then a SECOND postfix that TokenXP's single InvokeArg?
    cannot take (bear-trap 52). c1Run flips the subtraction, fId(qa + qb) - lim.
    The UnaryXP alternative leaves the grammar after the buy (expressionRecon,
    C1 BOUGHT IN PRINCIPLE).
***************************************************************************/

/***************************************************************************
    parseCode notes (Clod, 2026-10-05, after SEQ 300)
    compileIn drives the code on a floor that carries the owner and pops it, so
    parseCode needs no processingCodE pair and no currentMETHOD or lastIndenT
    bracket -- the 2026-10-04 BLOCKER is gone with them.
    The owner is `action`, as in processCode: the CodE for a rule, else the argument.
    compileIn answers true or false; the BlocK rides back on pcReq.result.
    Read it as `blk = pcReq.result;` then `*blk`. `*pcReq.result` CRASHES (exit 139):
    the star binds to pcReq, which holds nothing.
    Never `if blk;` -- truth-testing the BlocK fires it (bear-trap 34).
***************************************************************************/

